**What $\varphi$ computes.** The paper says only that $\varphi$ is a learnable MLP with an intra-block position encoding that maps the $l$ keys of a block to one compressed key, and that values get an analogous map. A common reading, used by the open PyTorch reimplementation native-sparse-attention-pytorch, is:

1. add a learned position vector to each of the $l$ keys, so the MLP knows which slot of the block each key came from;
2. flatten the $l \times d_k$ block into one vector of $l\,d_k$ numbers;
3. map it through a two-layer MLP to a single $d_k$-wide key.

With the paper's $l = 32$ that is 32 keys in and one key out, once every $d = 16$ tokens. Values go through a separate MLP of the same shape. Lesson 6 builds exactly this.
